 "source_type": "instagram",
 "source_url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DgIqfQYiTef/",
 "title": "Call out the guilt trip and leave an exit",
 "topic": "communication",
 "summary": "The creator explains how to handle someone using fear, obligation, or guilt (FOG) during an argument. He claims you should call out the behavior in a non-confrontational way and provide them a golden bridge to retreat.",
 "key_points": [
  "FOG stands for fear, obligation, or guilt.",
  "If someone uses FOG in an argument, call it out in a non-confrontational way.",
  "Give the other person an out so they can save face, known in interrogation training as a golden bridge.",
  "Example framing: \"Maybe you didn't mean to, but it sounded like you wanted me to feel guilty about working next week, and I know that you're a good person and don't think you meant it that way.\""
 ],
 "content": "Call out the guilt trip... Then leave them an exit.\nIn the art of war, they say give your opponent a golden bridge on which they can retreat. So if I'm in an argument, is someone using fear, obligation, or guilt? And that acronym spells FOG. And if someone is doing any of those things, I want to call it out in a non-confrontational way. So let's say, um, you give me some line about like if you don't do this, I'm going to have to work 19 hours next week. I can say, Stephen, maybe you didn't mean to, but it sounded like you wanted me to feel guilty about me working next week. And I know that you're a good person, I don't think you meant it that way. So I'm will absolutely call out any fear, obligation, or guilt, always. And I always want to use that word where it's non-confrontational and I want to give them an out. So in interrogation training, we always call this the golden bridge. In the art of war, they say give your opponent a golden bridge on which they can retreat.\nText on screen: Call out the guilt trip... Then leave them an exit.",
 "status": "done",
